cryptsetup
Usage: cryptsetup <action> [options] <device> [name]

Set up encrypted block devices. On xos this is what p3 -- the state
partition -- is built with, and it is the only way anything survives a
reboot.

This page documents the actions and options xos itself uses. cryptsetup
upstream has many more; these are the ones that matter here, and the ones
the curriculum drills.

actions
	luksFormat	write a new LUKS2 header, destroying what was there
	open		unlock a device and expose it as /dev/mapper/NAME
	close		lock it again and remove the mapping
	status		is NAME open, and with what cipher
	luksDump	print the header: cipher, key slots, kdf

	--type		luks2 (the default) or plain
	--integrity	add authentication as well as encryption
	--cipher	which cipher, e.g. aes-xts-plain64
	--key-size	key length in bits
	--pbkdf		which key-derivation function, argon2id or pbkdf2
	--batch-mode	never prompt or ask to confirm
	--readonly	open without allowing writes
	--verbose	say what is happening

Encryption alone is not enough. Plain LUKS gives confidentiality: an
attacker cannot READ your data. It does not stop them CHANGING it --
decrypting tampered ciphertext yields attacker-controlled garbage, which
the filesystem driver then parses as root. --integrity adds an
authentication tag per sector, so tampering is refused rather than
parsed. That is the same fail-closed property dm-verity gives the
read-only root, and it is why xos uses both.

	cryptsetup luksFormat --integrity hmac-sha256 /dev/sda3
	cryptsetup open /dev/sda3 state
	mount -o nosuid,nodev,noexec /dev/mapper/state /state
	cryptsetup close state

The passphrase is typed on a keyboard belonging to a machine you do not
own. Against a malicious BIOS or a hardware keylogger it is captured no
matter what this tool does. That is why p3 stays locked by default.

See also: mount, losetup, blkid, dd
